WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.261
The building block: an augmented model.

2
00:00:02.541 --> 00:00:13.080
Both workflows and agents are built from the same brick, which the Anthropic post calls the augmented LLM: a model with access to retrieval, tools, and memory.

3
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:18.032
You built most of one in Studio 1 (tool use) and Studio 2 (retrieval).

4
00:00:18.312 --> 00:00:23.432
The model can generate its own search queries, pick tools, and decide what to remember.

5
00:00:23.712 --> 00:00:31.456
Microsoft’s “AI Agents for Beginners” course describes the same thing in older vocabulary from classical AI.

6
00:00:31.736 --> 00:00:45.304
An agent has an environment it operates in (a booking system, a code repository, a project tracker), sensors that read the environment’s state (tools that look things up), and actuators that change it (tools that write).

7
00:00:45.584 --> 00:00:48.869
That framing is worth keeping, because it makes risk visible.

8
00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:51.624
Sensors can return wrong or hostile data.

9
00:00:51.904 --> 00:00:53.547
Actuators can do damage.

10
00:00:53.827 --> 00:00:58.093
When you design an agent later in this studio, you will list both explicitly.

11
00:00:58.373 --> 00:01:01.957
The Anthropic post’s advice on the brick itself is practical.

12
00:01:02.237 --> 00:01:08.125
Tailor the augmentations to your use case, and give the model a well-documented interface to each one.

13
00:01:08.405 --> 00:01:21.333
It also recommends starting with the model API directly rather than a framework, because many patterns take only a few lines of code, and frameworks can hide the prompts and responses you need to see when something goes wrong.

14
00:01:21.613 --> 00:01:24.579
If you do use one, understand what it does underneath.

15
00:01:24.859 --> 00:01:28.933
This week you will write every pattern by hand for exactly that reason.
